{
  "title": "Flyweight pattern",
  "idea": "The Flyweight pattern shares one immutable object for the state many items have in common, and passes the state that differs per item in from outside.",
  "points": [
    "Intrinsic state is shared and immutable; extrinsic state, like position, stays with each item.",
    "A factory caches flyweights by key, so equal requests return the same instance.",
    "The JDK does this: `Integer.valueOf` returns cached objects for small values.",
    "Use it for huge numbers of similar objects; for a few hundred, skip it."
  ],
