  "pitfalls": [
    "A setter on a shared flyweight changes every board at once; make all its fields final.",
    "Creating flyweights with `new` skips sharing; nest the factory and make the constructor private.",
    "Comparing boxed `Integer` values with `==` works for small numbers, fails for large; use `equals`."
  ],
  "quiz": {
    "q": "What does this print?",
    "code": "Integer a = -128, b = -128;\nInteger c = 128, d = 128;\nSystem.out.println((a == b) + \" \" + (c == d));",
    "lang": "java",
    "options": [
      "true true",
      "false false",
      "true false",
      "false true"
    ],
    "answer": 2,
    "why": "Autoboxing uses `Integer.valueOf`, which caches -128 to 127 by default. 128 is outside that range, so `c` and `d` are two distinct objects."
  },
  "takeaway": "Share the immutable common part once; pass the varying part in per call.",
